{"ts": 1789784244.5543082, "kind": "assistant_text", "text": "Nothing beats 0.910. The two co-leaders are [36, \u221222, +31] and [36, \u221222, \u221238]. Both are made of features that were individually strong and roughly independent \u2014 a good sign this isn't pure selection noise.\n\nI'll pick [36, \u221222, +31]: feature 31 had a stronger standalone Sharpe (+0.442) than 38 (\u22120.426), and its sign is positive-natural, so slightly less of the in-sample gain is likely to be selection artifact.\n\nNow calibrating the OOS forecast. I searched a large space: 80 single-sign screens, then pairs and triples from the top \u2014 effectively selecting the max over hundreds of implicitly-tested specs. The winner's 0.910 is therefore biased upward. A few considerations:\n\n- **Selection bias.** With n=5000 daily obs, the standard error of an annualized Sharpe is roughly \u221a(252/5000) \u2248 0.22. Taking a max over ~200+ correlated candidates inflates the winner by perhaps 1.5\u20132 SEs of the *effective* independent search width. But my search wasn't blind: the winning triple's components each had genuinely strong standalone signal, and the gains from combining (0.60 \u2192 0.81 \u2192 0.91) follow the smooth diversification pattern you'd expect from three real, weakly-correlated signals rather than the jumpy pattern of noise-mining. That argues the bulk of 0.91 is real.\n- **Shrinkage.** I'd discount the IS figure by roughly 25\u201330%, landing near 0.62\u20130.68. Signal decay OOS (regime drift, feature staleness) argues for a bit more haircut.\n- **Uncertainty.** The sampling SE alone is ~0.22; adding structural/regime risk, my posterior SD should be wider, ~0.28.\n\nI'll center my belief at **0.64** with SD **0.28**."}
