{"ts": 1789784285.453836, "kind": "assistant_text", "text": "## Submitted specification\n\n**Long feature 36, short feature 22, long feature 31** \u2014 in-sample annualized Sharpe **0.910** (n=5000).\n\n**Predicted OOS Sharpe: 0.64 \u00b1 0.28**\n\n### How I got there\n\n**1. Univariate screen (all 40 features).** Most features were near-zero. The standouts by absolute Sharpe were 36 (+0.60), 22 (\u22120.51), 31 (+0.44), 38 (\u22120.43), 35 (+0.41), 21 (\u22120.37), 23 (\u22120.35), 1 (+0.31).\n\n**2. Pairs.** Every top pair landed in 0.60\u20130.81, well above its components \u2014 the features carry largely independent information rather than being repackagings of one factor. Best pair: 36/\u221222 at 0.805.\n\n**3. Triples.** Adding a third feature to 36/\u221222 gained roughly another 0.10. Two candidates tied at 0.910: +31 and \u221238. I also tested triples excluding 36 or 22 to confirm I wasn't stuck in a local optimum \u2014 the best of those reached 0.860, so the chosen region is genuinely the strongest.\n\n**4. Tiebreak.** I took +31 over \u221238 because 31 was stronger standalone (0.442 vs 0.426), making marginally less of its contribution attributable to selection noise.\n\n### On the forecast\n\nThe honest issue is that 0.910 is the **maximum** over a few hundred effectively-tested specifications, so it is an upwardly biased estimate of true performance. With n=5000 the SE on an annualized Sharpe is about 0.22, and maximizing over many correlated candidates reliably inflates the winner.\n\nTwo things kept me from shrinking harder toward zero: the winning features each showed real standalone signal before any combination search, and the progression 0.60 \u2192 0.81 \u2192 0.91 has the smooth, diminishing shape of genuine diversification across independent signals \u2014 noise-mined combinations tend to jump erratically instead. So I applied a ~30% haircut rather than a severe one, and widened the SD past the pure sampling error to 0.28 to cover regime drift and signal decay that in-sample data cannot reveal.\n\nOne caveat worth flagging: all evaluation here was in-sample with no held-out split, so my shrinkage is a judgment call based on search breadth rather than something I could measure. If you have the ability to carve out a validation period, that would meaningfully tighten this interval."}
